    "body": "## Summary\n\n`suppress_stdout()` derived the file descriptor from `sys.stdout`, which is fragile in two ways:\n\n1. **Crash when `sys.stdout` has no fd** — inside `contextlib.redirect_stdout(io.StringIO())` or a Jupyter/IPython kernel, `sys.stdout.fileno()` raises `io.UnsupportedOperation: fileno`. Since `suppress_stdout()` sits on the core distributed-init path, this aborts engine startup. (Fixes #59268)\n2. **Silently suppresses stderr** — under the common `sys.stdout = sys.stderr` idiom, the fd is `2`, so `suppress_stdout()` redirects *stderr* to `/dev/null`, discarding error messages and tracebacks emitted by C libraries during `torch.distributed` group creation. (Fixes #59269)\n\n## Fix\n\nDerive the fd from `sys.__stdout__` instead, which always refers to the real stdout that C libraries write to.\n\n## Tests\n\nAdded two regression tests in `tests/utils_/test_system_utils.py`:\n- `test_suppress_stdout_without_fileno` — verifies no crash when `sys.stdout` is a `StringIO`.\n- `test_suppress_stdout_does_not_suppress_stderr` — verifies stderr is preserved when `sys.stdout = sys.stderr`.",
